\description{
Chao et al.'s (2005) abundance-based Jaccard dissimilarity, which
estimates the shares of individuals that belong to shared species,
including shared species not seen.
}
\details{
The Chao-Jaccard dissimilarity is defined as:
\deqn{1 - \frac{UV}{U + V - UV}}

with
\deqn{U = \min\left(1,\ \frac{C_X}{N_X} + \frac{N_Y - 1}{N_Y}\cdot\frac{f_{1}^{Y}}{2f_{2}^{Y}}\cdot\frac{S_X}{N_X}\right)}
and \eqn{V} the same with \eqn{X} and \eqn{Y} swapped.

Where:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{N_X}, \eqn{N_Y} : The total counts of each sample.
\item \eqn{C_X} : The counts in \eqn{X} of the features shared with \eqn{Y}.
\item \eqn{f_{1}^{Y}}, \eqn{f_{2}^{Y}} : The number of shared features with 1
and with 2 counts in \eqn{Y}. When \eqn{f_{2}^{Y} = 0}, 1 is used.
\item \eqn{S_X} : The counts in \eqn{X} of the shared features with 1 count
in \eqn{Y}.
}

It ranges from 0 to 1: 1 when the samples share no features (and \code{NaN}
when both are empty). It is not a metric.

It equals vegan's \code{vegdist(x, "chao")}. It is the Jaccard-type index;
vegan's \code{chaodist()} offers the Sorensen type and others. Named
\code{chao_jaccard()}, not \code{chao()}, to keep it apart from the alpha metric
\code{\link[=chao1]{chao1()}}; \code{beta_div(x, 'chao')} finds it.

